<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beni Hasan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beni Hasan is an ancient cemetery on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>, near Minya. Its rock-cut tombs of the Eleventh and Twelfth Dynasties belonged to the nomarchs of the Oryx <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a>, and their paintings show daily life, wrestling, warfare and a caravan of Asiatic traders in the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khnumhotep-ii">Khnumhotep II</a>. They are a major source for provincial society in the Middle Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beni-hasan.jpg" alt="Beni Hasan" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markh at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exterior_view_of_tombs_of_Khety_and_Barquet_III.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Beni Hasan is an ancient funerary site cut into the limestone cliffs on the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a>, on the east bank about 20 kilometres south of Minya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>. During the Middle Kingdom it was the main cemetery of the sixteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, the Oryx nome, whose capital was the town of Hebenu. Burials were made from the late Old Kingdom to the Middle Kingdom, between the end of the third millennium and the start of the second millennium BCE. The upper cemetery has 39 rock-cut tombs, chiefly of the Eleventh and Twelfth Dynasties, and a lower cemetery of about 900 graves, mainly shaft tombs, lies below.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Provincial governors, conventionally called nomarchs, were buried in the rock-cut tombs. In the Old Kingdom and the first half of the Middle Kingdom the office often appears to have been hereditary, with local dynasties holding the same province for generations, though the nomarch remained the king's representative. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> the great families seem to have stopped building immense funerary complexes; one scholar, Connor, cautions that this may reflect the surviving evidence or a change in how power was expressed rather than a decline of the families.</p><p>Of the upper tombs, twelve bear inscriptions: eight belonged to governors of the Oryx nome. The Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> recorded the paintings and inscriptions in four volumes published between 1893 and 1900, and John Garstang excavated 888 tombs of the lower cemetery in 1902–1904.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Twelve of the 39 upper tombs keep painted scenes of farming, crafts, hunting, games, fighting and the arrival of foreign groups. The tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khnumhotep-ii">Khnumhotep II</a>, Overseer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-ii">Senusret II</a>, has an inscription of 222 columns telling his life. On its north wall fifteen Aamu are shown, while the inscription records 37; their leader Abisharie bears the title &quot;ruler of the hill-lands&quot;. </p><p>The tomb of Baqet III has a wrestling cycle of 220 pairs, and that of Khety shows 122 pairs of wrestlers, military training and an assault on fortified places. Four tombs are open to visitors: those of Amenemhat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khnumhotep-ii">Khnumhotep II</a>, Baqet III and Khety. South of the cemetery <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> built a rock-cut temple to the goddess Pakhet, known as the Speos Artemidos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beni Hasan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A caravan of Asiatic traders is painted in the tomb of Khnumhotep II at <b>Beni Hasan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
